  "id": "DrugBank_DB11160 - MONDO_10481",
  "verbalization": "Phenyltoloxamine is implicated in the development of angioedema through a chain of indications that link it to urticaria, a condition associated with allergic reactions. The evidence suggests that Phenyltoloxamine may share a common pathway with other antihistamines like Rupatadine and Pheniramine, which are also linked to angioedema. These drugs are typically used to treat urticaria, and their association with angioedema implies a potential mechanism involving histamine modulation or allergic response regulation. The presence of multiple pathways connecting Phenyltoloxamine to urticaria and subsequently to angioedema suggests a possible role in allergic inflammation, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The integration of these indications highlights the importance of considering antihistamine class interactions in the context of allergic disorders.",
